A||||18||docprego||Headliner Replacement||||1||
Z||000000||docprego||04-04-2001||01:32 AM||||How difficult is it to replace the headliner in a 88 GT with sunroof?<P>What parts do I need to buy and from who?<P>The interior is gray.<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>docprego<BR> <A HREF="http://www.playitagain-games.com" TARGET=_blank>www.playitagain-games.com</A> <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_2.jpg">   <BR>White 1988 GT<BR>Sunroof, Power lumbar seat, Factory sub||||reg||1
Z||000001||Haze_Performance||04-04-2001||01:44 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by docprego:<BR><B>How difficult is it to replace the headliner in a 88 GT with sunroof?<P>What parts do I need to buy and from who?<P>The interior is gray.<P>Thanks<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Pep Boys.. Get a roll of the stuff called. Heads Up Headliner Kit & A can of Heads Up spray glue. This stuff looks KILLER.. It should only cost you about $40 total.<P>Good Luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ttttttt.gif">     </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>Pennock's Fiero Forum Members Photo Page</A><BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1431213" TARGET=_blank><BR>Photopoint Gallery</A>||||reg||14
Z||000002||docprego||04-04-2001||01:51 AM||||Haze, that surprised me-I expected a major operation.  Maybe I am asking about the wrong part?  I am talking about the fabric which covers the roof and goes all the way forward to the windshield.<P>Really 90% of it is in great condition, the area just at the edge by the windshield is ripped and there are 2-3 minor pencil eraser sized holes near the subwoofer controls.<P>Can you tell me more about this product at Pep Boys?<P>Appreciate the advice,<P><BR>------------------<BR>docprego<BR> <A HREF="http://www.playitagain-games.com" TARGET=_blank>www.playitagain-games.com</A> <BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_2.jpg">     <BR>White 1988 GT<BR>Sunroof, Power lumbar seat, Factory sub<P>[This message has been edited by docprego (edited 04-04-2001).]<P>Think I just found a link describing the stuff: <A HREF="http://store.yahoo.com/rodi/headrepheadf.html" TARGET=_blank>http://store.yahoo.com/rodi/headrepheadf.html</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by docprego (edited 04-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Haze_Performance||04-04-2001||02:02 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by docprego:<BR><B>Haze, that surprised me-I expected a major operation.  Maybe I am asking about the wrong part?  I am talking about the fabric which covers the roof and goes all the way forward to the windshield.<P>Really 90% of it is in great condition, the area just at the edge by the windshield is ripped and there are 2-3 minor pencil eraser sized holes near the subwoofer controls.<P>Can you tell me more about this product at Pep Boys?<P>Appreciate the advice,<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It comes in a long box.. 6 ft or 9 ft rolls.<BR> You only need the 6 ft. (Youll still have some left over) It comes in many colors. (Including the light or dark Fiero gray) Oreif has done it to his car & I must say it looks GREAT!! I just picked up a roll of black to do my '85. The fabric looks very close to the stock stuff & its even padded. Oreif told me that it was very easy to use & it should only take you about an hour to an hour and a half. <BR><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ttttttt.gif">     </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>Pennock's Fiero Forum Members Photo Page</A><BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1431213" TARGET=_blank><BR>Photopoint Gallery</A>||||reg||14
Z||000004||Black88GT||04-04-2001||02:04 AM||||Haze, so you just spray it on, apply the headliner, and thats it?  Sounds to easy.  My t-top needs the headliner replaced and I have some beechwood headliner laying around, could I use that or what I have to buy there stuff?<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD - MAFOA<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://88database.homestead.com/files/AF.gif">  <BR><I>"Once you go black, you never go back..."</I>||||reg||1
Z||000005||docprego||04-04-2001||02:10 AM||||Ok, let's take this a step further.<P>How do you go about removing the headliner to replace it?<P>Does it remove in one piece and then what are you recovering.  I am assuming it some sort of material that you simply recover..<P><P>------------------<BR>docprego<BR> <A HREF="http://www.playitagain-games.com" TARGET=_blank>www.playitagain-games.com</A> <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_2.jpg">   <BR>White 1988 GT<BR>Sunroof, Power lumbar seat, Factory sub||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||04-04-2001||02:12 AM||||The Heads-Up kit is great!!<BR>Just be careful when taking out your headliner so you don't crack it. Then just peel off the old fabric. Brush off the old foam with a stiff brush (don't push too hard)<BR>Spray the glue and apply CAREFULLY so you don't get creases. The Heads-up stuff is thicker than the "headliner fabric" that many fabric stores sells. Comes out better than new!<P>See pics!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Headliner.jpg"> <BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||04-04-2001||02:32 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by docprego:<BR><B>How do you go about removing the headliner to replace it?<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Remove sunvisors, Light panel, Plastic trim and sail panel trim, clothes hooks, Third brake light (If equipped), and the sunroof handle and trim (If equipped). Carefully remove it. Follow directions in previous post.<P><BR>OOPS I forgot, leave lots of extra material around the sunroof area because the rubber trim has to be tucked in with the cloth. Trim it with a razor blade after it is installed. <BR>(That is why the pic of the headliner with the new cloth has a small looking hole for the sunroof)<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||docprego||04-04-2001||02:34 AM||||WOW!  Thanks so much Oreif!  I couldn't visualize it, but those pictures speak volumes.<P>Looks awesome!<P>I just went out to the garage to check it out and couldn't figure out how to get the headliner off.  How exactly did you remove your's?<P>*Ok, I see how to get it out now.<P>Many thanks,<P><BR>------------------<BR>docprego<BR> <A HREF="http://www.playitagain-games.com" TARGET=_blank>www.playitagain-games.com</A> <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_2.jpg">    <BR>White 1988 GT<BR>Sunroof, Power lumbar seat, Factory sub<p>[This message has been edited by docprego (edited 04-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||04-04-2001||02:43 AM||||Sunvisors, Just take the screws out at the base.<BR>Light panel, pop out plastic lens' remove screws disconnect connectors.<BR>3rd brake light, remove screws and connector.<BR>Sunroof handle and clothes hooks, remove screws.<BR>Sail panel trim, Remove seat belt holder (T-50 torx I think) Screw in door jamb, CAREFULLY grasp top pull away and down. Extra caution, sometimes there is a tar like seal that gets on the panel from the rear window. CLEAN it off prior to replacing it. (No need to get new one dirty!) and use caution on the re-install so you don't rip the new headliner.<BR>Trim above window, Just pull carefully it's held up by clips.<BR>Sunroof, You'll see a plastic trim, pull it out of the groove.<BR>The headliner should now be loose. CAREFULLY work it out without bending it. Do it slowly and carefully. (If you crack it or it is already cracked, it can be repaired using fiberglass resin and some duct tape.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Carrolles||04-04-2001||08:30 AM||||If you can't find the kit you can get the fabric at most fabric stores.  I get mine at Hancock Fabrics in Birmingham, Al for about $7.50/yard.  I usually get 1-1/2 yards to hav enough to do the sunvisors.  I use 3m FOamFast74 spray glue.  It is made for foam backed fabrics.  The glue sprays on like silly string or like a spider web.  Spray the headliner board and the back of the fabric.  You'll have about ten minutes to position the fabric and work out the wrinkles before the glue sets up.<P>If you break the headliner board you can repair it by gluing a patch over it.  Some of the guys coat the headliner board to make it stronger, I haven't and have not had any problems.<P>Be careful when you re-install the side panels thet run from floor to ceiling.  They usually have black goop on the edge that will rub off onto your new headliner (learned this the hard way).<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||04-04-2001||10:18 PM||||dont use spray glue, it usually falls back down after a while. buy a $5.00 can of contact cement and spray it with spray gun or 2'' paint roller on fabric and headliner then stick it together. You can buy the factory type material at a Fabric shop for $5 or $6 dollars with the foam already attached, only need  a yard or so. we did Lisas headliner a couple weeks ago in gray. whole thing took less than 1 1/2 hours. Less than $10/$11 and will NEVER fall back down.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Phil||04-04-2001||11:00 PM||||Be prepared to run to your dealer to get some new clips for the "A" pillar moldings. Those suckers snap very easily.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Jimmy||04-05-2001||01:12 AM||||I have a completely destroyed headliner in my 87 GT T-TOP( 6 pieces!).  Does anybody have any idea on where I should start.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  I think I am going to a salvage yard to hunt for a hardtop headliner and maybe start there.  TIA <P>Jim <BR>87 GT T-TOP V8<BR>87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000014||Carrolles||04-05-2001||08:11 AM||||I've used the 3M Foamfast74 spray adhesive for the last 10 years and have never had one come down yet.  That stuff is made for this application.<P>Typical spray adhesive will let go after about a day.||||reg||1
Z||000015||skitime||04-05-2001||08:13 AM||||Jimmy I did my 88 T-top and It is unbelievable fragle it is no wonder yours broke into so many pieces.  Yes you probably should start with a hardtop headliner and recut the T-top shape. It is nearly impossible to keep the front together around the front interior light. If you lay and glue the panels seperatly it will be covered by the light fixture.  Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000016||rogergarrison||04-05-2001||11:42 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Carrolles:<BR><B>I've used the 3M Foamfast74 spray adhesive for the last 10 years and have never had one come down yet.  That stuff is made for this application.<P>Typical spray adhesive will let go after about a day.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>they must have improved it. I been doing them for 20 years, part of my business and used to do van conversions. Ive never had a spray can job sit out in the sun for long without falling down. I use the contact cement like the real upolstery guys. Its made for linoleum and formica counter tops. once it dries - its permanent. I cant afford to have them keep coming back. All my opinion from my own experience, but use what you want. Ill give you spray can is simpler.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Uaana||04-07-2001||01:47 PM||||Just one more question on this.<BR>Has anyone tried any different materials other than the original looking cloth? <BR>I was thinking of using vinyl instead for that faux leather look.||||reg||1
Z||000018||rogergarrison||04-07-2001||08:07 PM||||Yes, I just noticed the other day both sides are dirty from driving all summer with windows down and sucking up road dust. Nothing cleans the original type material without leaving water marks or dirt mark. Im recovering it next winter in vinyl.Im just going to put it over the cloth one so it will be padded too.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
